  "prediction": "**Summary of USAID Assistance to the West Bank and Gaza**\n\nThe United States Agency for International Development (USAID) has provided over $5 billion in bilateral assistance to the Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza since 1993. The assistance, primarily funded through the Economic Support Fund (ESF), aims to build a more democratic, stable, prosperous, and secure Palestinian society. As of March 31, 2018, USAID had obligated approximately $544.1 million (over 99 percent) of the $544.5 million allocated for the West Bank and Gaza in fiscal years 2015 and 2016.\n\nThe assistance was allocated towards various development objectives, including Economic Growth and Infrastructure, Investing in the Next Generation, and Governance and Civic Engagement. The largest share of the assistance, about 60 percent, supported Economic Growth and Infrastructure, which included projects such as the Architecture and Engineering Services project and the Rehabilitation of the Gaza Water and Wastewater System.\n\nUSAID also made direct payments to Palestinian Authority (PA) creditors, totaling about $544.1 million, to support the PA's debt relief. The agency vetted the creditors to ensure that the assistance did not inadvertently support terrorist activities. USAID officials stated that they do not plan to resume cash payments to the PA, citing concerns about transparency and accountability.\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) conducted a performance audit of USAID's assistance to the West Bank and Gaza and found that the agency complied with relevant laws and regulations, including antiterrorism policies. However, the audit identified weaknesses in the PA's financial management systems, including the Ministry of Health's medical procurement system and the Ministry of Finance and Planning's petroleum procurement system.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the significant investment made by the United States in the Palestinian people and the importance of ensuring that assistance is used effectively and efficiently to promote stability and prosperity in the region.",
